<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hurricane Mitch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hurricane Mitch was one of the deadliest Atlantic hurricanes on record, striking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> in late October and early November 1998. Its torrential rains caused floods and landslides that killed about 11,000 people, mostly in Honduras and Nicaragua, including some 2,000 in a mudslide on the Casita volcano. It destroyed much of the region's roads, bridges and crops, setting back the economies of Honduras and Nicaragua by years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hurricane-mitch.jpg" alt="Hurricane Mitch" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitch_1998-10-26_1845Z.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mitch began as Tropical Depression Thirteen on 22 October 1998 over the south-western Caribbean Sea, from a tropical wave that had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on 10 October. It became a hurricane on 24 October and rapidly intensified under warm waters and good outflow, reaching Category 5 on 26 October. At its peak it had sustained winds of 180 mph (290 km/h) and a minimum pressure of 905 millibars while off the northern coast of Honduras.</p><p>Forecasters had expected a turn north towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>. Instead the storm turned south because of a ridge that was not observed while it was active. It weakened over land and struck Honduras on 29 October with winds of 80 mph (130 km/h), then moved slowly westward through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Its remnants reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-campeche">Bay of Campeche</a>, redeveloped into a tropical storm on 3 November, crossed the Yucatán and made a final landfall near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, on 5 November. It became extratropical and was tracked until 9 November, when it passed north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Moving slowly between 29 October and 3 November, Mitch dropped historic amounts of rain on Honduras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua, with unofficial reports of up to 75 inches (1,900 mm). At least 11,374 people were confirmed killed and over 11,000 were left missing by the end of 1998, so the true toll is unknown. Most deaths came from floods and mudslides, and Honduras suffered over half of them. Roughly 2.7 million people were left homeless across the region, and total damage was estimated at about 6 billion dollars.</p><p>In Honduras the storm wrecked about 35,000 houses and damaged another 50,000. More than 70 percent of the transport infrastructure was damaged, mostly highways and bridges, and about 70 percent of the country lost its water sources. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> a large landslide affected three neighbourhoods and formed a temporary dam. The schooner Fantome of Windjammer Barefoot Cruises was lost with all hands off Honduras.</p><p>President Carlos Roberto Flores estimated that Mitch reversed 50 years of progress in Honduras. The economy contracted by 1.9 percent in 1999, and officials thought full reconstruction would take at least 15 years. The government imposed a curfew after reports of looting and restricted constitutional rights for 15 days. Some areas received no help until 12 November, and about 285,000 people were still in shelters three weeks after landfall. Outbreaks of cholera, leptospirosis and dengue followed.</p><p>Countries around the world gave aid totalling 6.3 billion dollars. The World Meteorological Organization retired the name Mitch, replacing it with Matthew for the 2004 season. It had been the deadliest Atlantic hurricane since the Great Hurricane of 1780, and the deadliest in Central American history, surpassing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-fifi">Hurricane Fifi</a> of 1974.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hurricane Mitch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hurricane Mitch</b> in October 1998 killed more than 5,000 Hondurans, destroyed much of the country’s infrastructure and set the economy back by years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
